        <td>Resolution, full-color imaging, and any optional add-on sensors determine which defects and foreign materials can be detected.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Carryover</td>
        <td>Good product escaping into the reject stream is a direct yield loss; low carryover shortens the payback period of the investment.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Service network in Turkey</td>
        <td>When a line stops mid-season, waiting for a technician from abroad means days of lost production.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Spare parts stock</td>
        <td>Having ejectors, valves, lighting, and electronic boards stocked in Turkey keeps downtime to a minimum.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Written performance commitment</td>
        <td>Seeing the expected sorting accuracy and carryover for your own product stated in the quotation is a far more reliable basis for a decision than catalog figures.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>One point worth making clear: a color sorter works on differences in color and appearance. For foreign material that is the same color as the product — glass, metal, or stone — or for objects embedded inside the product, the right solution is an <a href="/en/x-ray-food-inspection/">X-ray food inspection</a> system. In many facilities, the two technologies run in sequence and complement each other.</p>

